<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hatikvah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">poem first written <a href="/years/1877">1877</a>, text dated <a href="/years/1878">1878</a>, published <a href="/years/1886">1886</a> in Barkai; set to music 1887/1888</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as הַתִּקְוָה</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naftali Herz Imber, a Jewish poet from Złoczów (Zolochiv) in Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, wrote the first version of his poem in 1877 while staying with a Jewish scholar in Iași, and its text is dated 1878. The nine-stanza poem, &quot;Tikvatenu&quot; (&quot;Our Hope&quot;), set out his thoughts after the founding of the settlement of Petah Tikva and expressed the wish of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the diaspora to return to the Land of Israel.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Imber emigrated to Ottoman-ruled Palestine in 1882 and read his poem to the pioneers of the early Jewish villages: Rishon LeZion, Rehovot, Gedera and Yesud Hama'ala. Shmuel Cohen, a very young resident of Rishon LeZion with a musical background, sang the poem to a melody he knew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, after seeing the emotional response of the farmers who had heard it. His adaptation helped the poem spread quickly through the Zionist communities of Palestine, and it was adopted as an anthem by the Hovevei Zion and later by the Zionist Movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The melody is based on &quot;La Mantovana&quot;, a 16th-century Italian song composed by Giuseppe Cenci (Giuseppino del Biado) around 1600. It circulated widely in Renaissance Europe under other titles, among them the Polish &quot;Pod Krakowem&quot; and the Romanian &quot;Cucuruz cu frunza-n sus&quot;. Its best-known earlier use was by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedrich-smetana">Bedřich Smetana</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vltava">Vltava</a>, from Má vlast. The tune follows a minor scale, which is uncommon in national anthems and often heard as mournful, although the title and words are optimistic. The first line of the refrain, &quot;Our hope is not yet lost&quot;, has been compared with the openings of the Polish and Ukrainian anthems, and may allude to the vision of the dried bones in Ezekiel 37.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Zionist Organization held anthem competitions in 1898 and 1900 and judged all the entries unsatisfactory. Imber's poem was popular nonetheless, and at the Sixth Zionist Congress in 1903 it was sung by those opposed to the proposal for a Jewish state in Uganda. Only at the Eighteenth Congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1933 did a motion formally adopt it as the movement's anthem. The British Mandate government briefly banned its public performance from 1919.</p><p>In 1948 the song was unofficially proclaimed the anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israel">State of Israel</a>. It became official only in November 2004, when the Knesset sanctioned an abbreviated version, the first stanza and refrain. Some religious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> have objected that it never mentions God or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, and some non-Jewish citizens of Israel, particularly Arab citizens, find that its focus on Jewish aspirations makes it hard to identify with.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hatikvah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Eighteenth Zionist Congress in Prague formally adopted &quot;<b>Hatikvah</b>&quot; as the movement's anthem in 1933.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hatikvah">Hatikvah</a></li><li><blockquote>It is often contrasted with the national anthem, &quot;<b>Hatikvah</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/jerusalem-of-gold">Jerusalem of Gold</a></li></ul></section>
